.\" Copyright (C) 2026  the sl Go port authors
.\" Based on the Tcl script by Thomas A. Phelps, Copyright (c) 2011-2012.
.\" GPL-3.0-or-later. No warranty.
.TH SL 1 "2026-10-21" "sl 1.1.4" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sl \- usefully organize directories and point out interesting files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sl
.RI [ option ...]
.RI [ file | directory ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sl
takes the most common use of
.BR ls (1)
\(em displaying the files in a directory compactly in several columns \(em and
makes it substantially more useful.
.PP
It does four things that
.B ls
does not:
.TP
.B Grouping
Files are sorted by purpose, not by name: headers, source code, web pages,
documents, books, images, audio, video, fonts, data, archives and Apple II disk
images each get their own block, in a fixed order. A directory is classified by
what it contains, so a folder full of
.B .mp3
is shown as audio material.
.TP
.B Marking
Entries that stand out from the rest of the directory are marked: relatively
large, recently changed, recently read, empty, unreadable, world-writable,
hard-linked, a broken link, odd permission bits, or part of a name series.
The thresholds are relative \(em \(lqthe biggest files here\(rq, not \(lqbigger
than 1 MiB\(rq \(em because a directory that is all movies and a directory that
is all Makefiles have different ideas of \(lqlarge\(rq.
.TP
.B Condensing
Runs of numbered names
.RB ( frame200 .dng ", " frame201 .dng ", ...)"
collapse into one line
.RB ( frame200.dng...342 ),
and a shared prefix can be elided so that a column of
.B myproject-notes
and
.B myproject-todo
shows only the differing tails.
.TP
.B Layout
Column widths are computed per column rather than per directory, names are
shortened in the middle when the screen is narrow (keeping the first characters,
the number and the suffix), and a group title that would land at the bottom of a
column is moved to the top of the next one.
.PP
.B sl
is meant for a human looking at a directory. It is not a replacement for
.BR ls (1)
in scripts: the output is coloured, adaptive and full of heuristics.
.SH OPTIONS
Options are spelled the GNU way: two dashes for a long name
.RB ( "\-\-width" ),
one dash for a short one
.RB ( "-w" ).
Values may be written as
.BR "\-\-width=100" ,
.BR "\-\-width 100" ,
.BR "-w 100"
or
.BR "\-w100" .
The original Tcl script also accepted long names behind a single dash
.RB ( "\-width 100" ).
This port does not: one dash always means a short option, and nothing else.
A Tcl\-style spelling is rejected with a hint instead of being guessed at,
so
.B \-only
is never silently split into
.BR \-o " " \-n " " \-l " " \-y :
.PP
.RS 4
sl: unknown option: \-only (did you mean \-\-only?)
.RE
.SS Selection
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
Show everything, including dotfiles and files that are normally ignored
(describes the same as
.BR "\-\-ignore 0" ).
.TP
.BI \-\-ignore " N"
Show entries whose ignore level is at least
.IR N .
Level 3 (the default) hides editor and build droppings
.RB ( .o ", " .pyc ", " CVS ", " TAGS ),
level 2 also shows C headers
.RB ( .h ),
level 1 also shows dotfiles, level 0 shows everything.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-only
Show only entries that carry some signal: changed, read, warned about, or
annotated. Useful in a large or familiar directory. This implies
.BR "\-\-relsize 0 \-\-series 0 \-\-prefix 0 \-\-summary 0"
and turns
.B \-\-title
off, so put it last on the command line if you want those.
.SS Output
.TP
.BR \-1 ", " \-\-one
One entry per line.
.TP
.BR \-w " N" ", " \-\-width " N"
Assume the terminal is
.I N
columns wide instead of asking the terminal.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-title
Print prettified names: camelCase becomes spaced words, dots, underscores and
dashes become spaces. The name you see is then not exactly the name you must
type, which is why this is off by default.
.TP
.B \-\-nosfx
With
.BR \-\-title ,
drop the suffix for audio, video, image and font files.
.TP
.BR \-\-owner-notes ", " \-\-no-owner-notes
In your home directory, name the owner of regular files with a single hard link
that do not belong to you, for example
.BR owner=root .
The point is to notice leftovers from
.BR root ,
from a container, or from another account inside your own home. It is on by
default because it costs nothing and says nothing unless there is something to
say; outside the home directory it never appears. Switch it off with
.BR \-\-no-owner-notes .
.TP
.B \-\-group-mix
When a directory has been classified by its contents, print how many entries
actually matched the guess instead of the plain count:
.B 5+3
means 5 of 8 entries are images, so the label is a 5-to-3 decision and not a
unanimous one. Diagnostic; off by default.
.TP
.B \-\-experimental
Convenience alias that turns on
.B \-\-group-mix
and
.B \-\-owner-notes
together. This is the block of code the original called TAP (the initials of the
author, Thomas A. Phelps): it was switched on by the existence of
.IR ~/prj/Multivalent ,
a directory used purely as a flag meaning \[lq]this is my machine\[rq].
.TP
.B \-\-inline-singleton
Show the lone entry of a directory instead of a count: a directory
.B src
that holds only
.B main.c
is printed as
.BR src/main.c
rather than
.BR src/1 .
The original script had this branch disabled
.RB ( "elseif {0 && $size==1}" );
it is available here, off by default, because long names can upset the columns.
.TP
.BI \-\-sort " KEY"
Order the entries inside each group. The keys are
.BR name " (the default: dictionary order), " size ", " time " (modification time) and " atime
(access time). The largest entry comes first for
.BR size ,
the most recently modified one for
.BR time ,
the most recently read one for
.BR atime .
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
  other                  other                 other
   draft.md 293K          todo.md               notes.md
   readme.md 88K          readme.md             todo.md
   notes.md               notes.md              readme.md
   todo.md                draft.md              draft.md
  \-\-sort size           \-\-sort time          \-\-sort atime
.EE
.RE
.PP
Four rules make the result mean something:
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-sort size
turns on
.B \-\-relsize 100
by itself, because with the default 5% only the largest few entries carry a
number and the order then looks random. An explicit
.B \-\-relsize
wins, and so does
.BR \-o ,
which switches size marks off on purpose.
.IP \(bu
Directories keep name order under
.BR \-\-sort size .
The size of a directory here is the number of entries in it, not the number of
bytes below it, so sorting by it would claim that a folder of many small files
outweighs a single large video. Sort them by
.B time
if you want them ordered by anything at all.
.IP \(bu
.B \-\-prefix
is off for every order except
.BR name :
a shared prefix is elided against the entry above, and after a sort that
neighbour is somebody else, so the shown names would lose a prefix that the
first one no longer displays.
.IP \(bu
Grouping is untouched: a sort reorders entries inside a group and never moves
one to another group.
.BR \-\-series
collapsing also happens first, on name order, and a collapsed run keeps the
place it was given.
.PP
With
.B \-\-nogroup
there is only one list, so the directories go first in name order and everything
else follows by the key.
.TP
.B \-\-nogroup
One flat list sorted by name, without group titles.
.TP
.BI \-\-ellipsis " S"
Text inserted where a name was cut (default
.RB \(dq...\(dq).
It only matters when names are actually being cut, which is
.BR \-\-shorten \'s
decision, not its own: pick a single character
.RB ( \(dq~\(dq ", " \(dq»\(dq )
when the terminal is narrow and every column counts.
See
.B CUTTING NAMES
below for what happens before the ellipsis is used.
.SS Thresholds
.TP
.BI \-\-relsize " N"
Mark the top
.I N
percent of entries by size (default 5, 0 disables).
The mark is the size itself
.RB ( "4.2M" ),
and it is relative to this directory: in a directory of ten files the single
largest one is marked at the default setting, in a directory of two thousand
the top hundred are. Set
.B \-\-relsize 100
to show the size of every entry, smallest included; note that
.B \-\-sort size
does this for you (see
.BR \-\-sort ).
Below 100 the cutoff is clamped to the median, so a directory in which everything
is nearly the same size produces no size marks at all: that is the original's
rule, kept because otherwise \[lq]all created a minute apart\[rq] would light up.
.TP
.BI \-\-relchange " N"
Mark the top
.I N
percent by modification time (default 10, 0 disables).
The mark reads
.B <2h
or
.BR <90d :
it means \*qchanged that recently\*q, not \*qchanged at that time\*q.
.TP
.BI \-\-relread " N"
Mark the top
.I N
percent by access time (default 10, 0 disables; set 0 on file systems mounted
with
.B noatime
or on macOS, where Spotlight makes every access time recent).
The mark reads
.BR \-5m .
Because a listing itself updates the access time of the directories it reads,
this is about files, and on Linux the
.B relatime
mount option is enough to make it useful.
.TP
.BI \-\-atime " N" ", \-\-age " N"
Additionally mark files read within the last
.I N
seconds. The name
.B \-\-age
is the one used on the original web page; the Tcl script called it
.BR \-atime .
.TP
.BI \-\-series " N"
Collapse a run of at least
.I N
numbered names (default 5, 0 disables).
A run is three or more names that are identical except for one run of digits or
one index letter, and that are adjacent after sorting:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
  video
  episode 01.mkv <2020 1000...8
.EE
.RE
.PP
The line above is eight files; the number after the dots is how many were
collapsed, and the size, when shown, is that of the first of them.
.PP
There is one inherited exception, and it surprises people: a run is
.B not
collapsed when its entries carry a mark of their own (a size, a
.BR <2h ,
a warning). This is the Tcl original\'s rule, kept on purpose \- the mark is the
reason the file was worth pointing out \- but it means a directory of
identical-looking fresh zero-byte files shows every name.
.B \-\-series 0
turns collapsing off entirely.
.TP
.BI \-\-prefix " N"
Elide a common prefix of at least
.I N
characters (default 0, i.e. off; 3 is what the author recommended).
The prefix is replaced by spaces, so the names still line up under each other
and only the differing tails remain:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
  myproject-plan.txt <NOW
           -todo.txt <NOW
.EE
.RE
.PP
Two details that decide whether it fires at all: the entries must be adjacent
in the listing (so it works inside a group, not across groups), and it runs only
in the groups where file names share structure \- directories, documents,
images, audio, video, fonts, archives.
.TP
.BI \-\-summary " N"
Print the summary line when at least
.I N
entries are shown (default 2, 0 disables).
.TP
.BI \-\-expando " N"
After listing, also list the single subdirectory if the directory holds at most
.I N
files (default 2, 0 disables).
.PP
This is not recursion. It unfolds a \*qsingle-child chain\*q: a directory that
holds a couple of files and exactly one subdirectory, which itself holds one
subdirectory, and so on \- the shape of
.BR project/project-1.0/src ,
where three levels hold nothing but the way down. Every level is listed with
its own grouping, marks and summary:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
  build          dev
  README <NOW     src/3 <NOW
  1 file (2B), 1 directory (3 files)

autoexpand src/:
  deep/1 <NOW    dev                util.c <NOW
                  main.c 2B <NOW
  2 files (4B), 1 directory (1 file)

autoexpand deep/:
  dev
   a.c 2B <NOW
.EE
.RE
.PP
As soon as a level holds a second subdirectory, or more than
.I N
files, the unfolding stops there. There is no depth flag and no breadth-first
walk; a listing of a whole tree is a different feature and is not implemented.
Use
.B \-\-expando 0
to turn it off.
.SS Colours and layout
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-color "[=\fIWHEN\fR]"
Colour output.
.I WHEN
is
.B auto
(the default: colour only when standard output is a terminal),
.B always
or
.BR never .
A bare
.B \-c
or
.B \-\-color
means
.BR always .
The value must be attached with an equals sign
.RB ( "\-\-color=never" ); a separate word would be indistinguishable from a
file name.
.TP
.B \-\-no-color
The same as
.BR \-\-color=never .
The environment variable
.B NO_COLOR
also turns colour off.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-log
Print the layout decisions that were taken (how many columns were tried, why one
shape was chosen over another). Intended for debugging, and it is what the Tcl
version's
.B \-log
did.
.TP
.BI \-\-colsep " N"
Minimum gap between columns (default 1).
.TP
.BI \-\-namemin " N"
Width that still leaves room for two columns (default 40).
.TP
.B \-\-k1000
Use 1000, not 1024, as the divisor for the
.BR K/M/G/T
suffixes.
.TP
.B \-\-k1024
Use 1024 as the divisor (the default; the counterpart of
.BR \-\-k1000 ).
.TP
.BI \-\-autosearch " RE"
Highlight, in every name shown, the part that matches the regular expression
.IR RE .
The default is the pattern the original used,
.BR "(?i)\eW(urgent|password|pw|live)\eW" .
An empty value disables the search. Version 1.1.4 fixed this option: the value
was validated all along, but the built-in pattern was what actually ran, so
.B \-\-autosearch
behaved like an on/off switch; a pattern that does not compile is now an error
instead of a silent fallback.
.TP
.BI \-\-rule " RE -> GROUP"
Force the group
.I GROUP
on every name that matches
.IR RE .
Repeatable, first match wins. See
.B RULES
below for the syntax and the list of groups.
.TP
.B \-\-notable
Highlight the names worth seeing at a glance (default on).
.TP
.B \-\-no-notable
Turn that highlighting off.
.TP
.BI \-\-shorten " N"
Start shortening names when the listing has at least
.I N
entries (default 10, 0 disables).
.SS Hooks
.TP
.BI \-\-annotate " CMD"
Run
.I CMD
through
.BR sh \-c
for every listed entry and append the first line of its output to the entry.
The path is available as
.B $1
and the name as
.BR $2 .
This replaces the
.B perfile
and
.B vc-*
hooks of the Tcl version, which required an embedded interpreter.
.TP
.BI \-\-annotate-batch " CMD"
Run
.I CMD
once for the whole directory instead of once per entry. The paths are written to
its standard input, one per line, and it must answer with one line per path; the
.IR i "-th answer annotates the " i "-th path, after the same trimming as"
.BR \-\-annotate .
The directory being listed is passed as
.BR $1 .
.PP
.RS
This is the cheap way to do what the Tcl version's
.B perfile
did, because Tcl called a procedure and a static binary has to start a process.
On a directory of 1200 files
.B \-\-annotate
starts 1200 shells \(em about 1.5 s on the machine this was measured on \(em
and
.B \-\-annotate-batch
starts one, about 31 ms.
.RE
.PP
Two consequences, both inherent. The command has to be a filter (it reads names
and writes answers) rather than a script that inspects one entry, and a name
containing a newline cannot be sent at all: such entries are left unannotated.
Answers are positional, so a command that prints a header, a warning or one
answer for several paths would shift everything by one: if the number of lines
differs from the number of paths, sl says so once on standard error and shows no
annotations for that directory at all. The whole run has a 30 second deadline.
.B \-\-annotate-batch
and
.B \-\-annotate
cannot be used together.
.TP
.BI \-\-annotate-match " RE"
Only annotate entries whose name matches the regular expression
.IR RE .
.TP
.BI \-\-mounts " LIST"
Colon- or comma-separated mount points that may need to spin up
(default
.BR /Volumes:/run/media:/media:/mnt ).
A symlink into one of them is shown as
.B \-\->
and is not stat()ed, so that a sleeping USB disk does not stall the listing.
.SS Miscellaneous
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
Read configuration from
.I FILE
instead of the default location.
.TP
.B \-\-no-config
Do not read any configuration file.
.TP
.BI \-\-startup " FILE"
Ignored, with a warning. This is the flag the Tcl version used to name its
startup script; a static binary cannot run Tcl, so the equivalent settings live
in the configuration file (see
.BR \-\-config ).
The flag exists so that old command lines keep working.
.SH RULES
The Tcl original could be extended by redefining procedures in
.BR ~/.sl.tcl ,
i.e. by running arbitrary code inside the listing. A static binary cannot host
an interpreter, so the same job is done with declarative rules: any number of
lines of the form
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
rule = \fIRE\fR -> \fIGROUP\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
in the configuration file, or the same text with
.BR \-\-rule " \fIRE\fR -> \fIGROUP\fR" .
A rule says "if the name matches
.IR RE ,
this entry belongs to
.IR GROUP ".
Nobody has to fetch a fifteen-year-old
.B ~/.sl.tcl
from a web archive to rename a group any more.
.SS Syntax
The left side is a Go regular expression. It is
.B unanchored
(the pattern is searched for, as in
.BR grep )
and
.B case-sensitive
unless it starts with
.BR (?i) .
The right side is the name of a group, one of:
.PP
.RS 4
.BR special ", " dir ", " build ", " app ", " dev ", " A2 ", " WWW ", " aux ,
.BR doc ", " book ", " document ", " image ", " vector ", " audio ", " video ,
.BR font ", " data ", " other ", " archive ", " dotfile ", " Mac ,
.BR file ", " ignore .
.RE
.PP
.B ignore
hides the entry the way a built-in ignorable name is hidden (it comes back with
.BR \-\-all ),
so a rule can also be used to suppress noise. The same list is printed by an
invalid rule:
.BR "sl: rule ...: unknown group" .
.SS What the pattern is matched against
Not the raw name, but the
.B classification name
\(em the entry's name with a compression wrapper stripped:
.B foo.1.gz
is matched as
.BR foo.1 ,
because that is what every other name test in the program sees. The match is
against the name only, never against the directory it lives in.
.SS Order and precedence
Rules are read top to bottom and the
.B first match wins.
A matching rule overrides everything the built-in tables decided \(em suffixes,
exact names, README/Makefile detection, the man-page rule for
.BR man1/foo.1 .
The one thing a rule cannot undo is the type of an entry: a directory stays a
directory for the purpose of what is printed, only the group label changes.
.SS Examples
.RS 4
.EX
# hide build leftovers instead of seeing them as "other"
rule = \\.(tmp|bak|swp)$ -> ignore

# my camera writes IMG_0001.JPG; the built-in table has only lowercase .jpg
rule = ^IMG_[0-9]+\\.JPG$ -> image

# treat .md as documentation
rule = \\.(md|rst)$ -> doc

# everything under this name is a project file
rule = (?i)^(makefile|cmakelists\\.txt)$ -> build

# MusicBrainz sidecars
rule = \\.(m3u|m3u8)$ -> audio
.EE
.RE
.SS Errors
A rule that does not parse, names an unknown group or contains a broken regular
expression stops the program with a message naming the offending line, at
startup rather than in the middle of a listing. Rules are cheap: each one is
one compiled regular expression, matched once per entry after the built-in
classification has run.
.SS The old file
.B ~/.sl.tcl
itself is not read, and cannot be: it is Tcl code.
.B \-\-startup
is accepted for compatibility and prints a warning; the configuration file is
its replacement. The rest of the original's customisation (
.BR perfile ", " vc-* )
has the same substitute:
.BR \-\-annotate .
.SH "COMPATIBILITY"
Two rules of the original are wrong in ways that show up on a normal Unix
system. Both are fixed by default, and both old rules are one flag away.
.TP
.B \-\-legacy-classify
Classify the contents of a directory by name length instead of by type: an entry
with no suffix is counted as a subdirectory. The original needed this because it
did not want to
.B stat
thousands of entries, but on Unix a name without a suffix is usually a file
.RB ( README ", " Makefile ", " LICENSE ),
so the rule turned ordinary files into imaginary subdirectories. The default is
to use the type that
.BR readdir (3)
already returns
.RB ( d_type ).
.TP
.B \-\-legacy-series
Accept the original's definition of a name series: two names of the same length
that differ in any one digit or in one run of letters, so
.B abc.txt
and
.B abd.txt
collapse. The default is stricter: the names must be identical except for one
run of digits
.RB ( episode01 ", " episode02 )
or for a single index letter that ends the stem
.RB ( part\ a ", " part\ b ).
.SH NOTATION
Each entry is a cell: an optional indent, the name, then zero or more marks.
Marks come in a fixed order and always immediately after the name (a link keeps
its arrow first):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
  Makefile <-W<90d     hard link, world-writable, changed 90 days ago
  empty.txt0           empty file
  src/2                a directory holding two files
  broken-link->X       a symbolic link that goes nowhere
  4.2M                 relatively large: the size is the mark
  chapter1.txt...6     six names collapsed into one (see \-\-series)
.EE
.RE
.PP
A listing is a grid, and a grid fills downwards: the first column is read from
the top to the bottom, then the second one, and so on. That is the order the
entries are in, so a sorted listing reads down a column and never across a line.
Push it into one column with
.B \-1
if the order matters more than the shape:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
  aaa/1        image        a.png 195K    other           readme.md 88K    todo.md
  zzz/5         c.png 586K   b.png         draft.md 293K   notes.md
.EE
.RE
.PP
read down: aaa, zzz, image, c.png, a.png, b.png, other, draft.md, readme.md,
notes.md, todo.md. Reading the same grid left to right would mix the groups up.
.PP
The marks answer three different questions, and it helps to know which one you
are looking at:
.TP
.B type
.BR / " (directory), " \-> " (link), " *
(executable), and the group title above the names.
.TP
.B anomaly
something to look at or clean up:
.B 0
(empty),
.B X
(broken),
.B <-
(another name points at this file),
.BR u " / " g " / " s
(odd permission bits),
.BR W
(world-writable),
.B r
(not readable),
.B x
(directory not searchable).
.TP
.B relative
something that stands out in \fIthis\fR directory:
.BR 4.2M ", " <2h ", " \-5m .
These are percentages, not absolutes, and they change as the directory does.
.TP
.B /
directory
.TP
.B \-\->
symbolic link pointing into another mount point (not stat()ed)
.TP
.B \->
symbolic link; a broken link keeps the arrow and gets
.B X
.TP
.B _
special file: socket, FIFO, device
.TP
.B *
executable file
.TP
.B 0
empty file, or an empty directory
.TP
.B 1
directory holding exactly one entry
.TP
.B <\-
more than one hard link to this file
.TP
.B X
broken link, or an abandoned editor/version-control backup
.TP
.B u
setuid bit;
.B g
setgid bit;
.B s
sticky bit
.TP
.B W
writable by everybody
.TP
.B r
not readable by the current user
.TP
.B x
directory is not searchable (no execute permission) by the current user
.TP
.B <2h
modified recently; the relative form means among the most recently modified
entries here
.TP
.B \-5m
read recently
.TP
.B 4.2M
relatively large
.TP
.B ...7
seven consecutive names were collapsed into this line
.SH "CUTTING NAMES"
Names are cut only when cutting buys something: when the listing still has to
fit a column budget. That is
.BR \-\-shorten \'s
job (default: from 10 entries on), and it works in three steps, cheapest first,
stopping as soon as the name fits:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
1. articles are dropped   the, a, an
2. prepositions become /  of, from, for, in, to, and, volume, vol
3. the middle is elided, keeping the head, the number and the tail
.fi
.RE
.PP
So a long name is not simply truncated \- it is first rewritten, and
.B the
.I shown
name may therefore differ from the real one:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
The Making of a Documentary about the Sea volume 001 of the series.txt
Making/Documentary about Sea/001/series.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
That is the Tcl original\'s behaviour, kept because it really does fit long
library names into a column, but it is worth knowing about before you type a
name from the screen.
.B \-\-shorten 0
turns all three steps off and shows names exactly as they are on disk, at the
cost of width.
.B \-\-ellipsis
changes only the text of step 3.
.SH GROUPS
In order of appearance:
.BR dir ,
.BR build ,
.BR app ,
.BR dev ,
.BR A2
(Apple II disk images: .dsk .do .po .2mg .2img .shk .sdk .sea .bsq .bxy .bny .bsc
\&.dc and, in this port, .woz .hdv .d13 .edsk .edd .nb2 .n35 .d16),
.BR WWW ,
.BR aux ,
.BR doc ,
.BR book ,
.BR document ,
.BR image ,
.BR vector ,
.BR audio ,
.BR video ,
.BR font ,
.BR data ,
.BR other ,
.BR archive ,
.BR dotfile ,
.BR Mac ,
.BR ignore .
A file whose name ends in
.B .nib
is treated as an Apple II diskette only when its size is exactly 232960 bytes
(a 35-track nibble image) or 116480 bytes (13-sector), because the same suffix
also belongs to NeXT/Interface Builder bundles.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/sl/config
User configuration. Defaults to
.IR ~/.config/sl/config .
It is created, fully commented out, on the first run; an existing file is never
overwritten.
.B \-\-no-config
skips both reading and creating it, and
.B \-\-config
.I FILE
names a file that must already exist.
.PP
Shell completion files are installed by the packages:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
/usr/share/bash-completion/completions/sl
/usr/share/zsh/site-functions/_sl
/usr/share/fish/vendor_completions.d/sl.fish
.fi
.RE
.PP
They are generated from the option table by
.BR "make completions" ,
not by the program at run time, and a test compares them with that table, so
they cannot silently fall behind.

.SH CONFIGURATION
On the first run
.B sl
writes a template to
.IR ~/.config/sl/config
in which every setting is present but commented out, together with its default;
the file therefore changes nothing until something in it is uncommented. Delete
it to get a fresh one at the next start.
.PP
The file is plain data, one
.I key = value
per line; a
.B #
starts a comment. Every long option can be used as a key, and three families are
specific to the file:
.PP
.nf
    # settings, spelled like the command line options
    ignore = 2
    relread = 0
    series = 3
    prefix = 3
    title = on
    ellipsis = ...

    # colours: SGR attributes, without the leading ESC[
    style.dir = 01;34
    style.exec = 01;32
    style.group = 01;35;4
    style.warning = 01;31

    # extra suffixes for an existing group
    group.doc.extra = .md .rst
    group.A2.extra = .po2
.fi
.PP
Command line options override the file. Colour names may be used instead of
numbers
.RB ( red ", " green ", " bright-blue ", " bold ", " underline ", ...).
.PP
Two keys are not spelled like an option and exist only here:
.B k
takes the number
.B 1000
or
.BR 1024 ,
and
.B rule
takes a user rule (see
.BR RULES ).
.B rule
may appear any number of times, and rules from the command line are matched
before the ones from the file \(em the command line wins, as it does everywhere
else.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set, colour is disabled (unless
.B \-\-color
is given).
.TP
.B LS_COLORS
If set, the standard keys
.BR di ,
.BR ln ,
.BR ex ,
.B and or
.BR or ,
as well as per-extension entries such as
.BR *.c=01;32 ,
override the built-in palette. Keys without a counterpart in
.B sl
are ignored.
.TP
.B COLUMNS
Terminal width to assume when standard output is not a terminal.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Configuration root; the file is read from
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/sl/config .
.SH EXAMPLES
The output below is real: it comes from running these commands on the directory
this snippet builds.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
mkdir demo && cd demo && mkdir season1
printf 'int main(void){return 0;}\n' > main.c
printf 'int util(void){return 1;}\n' > util.c
touch main.o
head -c 40000 /dev/urandom > cover.png
for i in 1 2 3; do head -c 900000 /dev/urandom > "track 0$i.mp3"; done
for i in 1 2 3 4 5 6; do echo x > "chapter$i.txt"; done
for i in 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8; do head -c 250000 /dev/urandom > "season1/episode 0$i.mkv"; done
echo '# readme' > README.md; echo 'all:' > Makefile
: > empty.txt; ln -s missing-target broken-link; mkdir world; chmod 777 world
echo x > .hidden
touch -d 2020-01-01 chapter*.txt
.EE
.RE
.SS "What is in here"
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sl --width 100
  world/0W <NOW    doc                 audio                      other
  build            chapter1.txt...6     track 01.mp3 879K <NOW    README.md <NOW
  Makefile <NOW    empty.txt0 <NOW     track 02.mp3 <NOW          broken-link->X <NOW
  dev              image               track 03.mp3 <NOW
  main.c <NOW      cover.png <NOW      video
  util.c <NOW                          season1/8 <NOW
  15 files (2.6M) + 1 .dotfile + 1 ignored, 2 directories (8 files)
.EE
.RE
.PP
Two source files in
.BR dev ,
six chapters collapsed into one line, three tracks in
.BR audio ,
the episode directory named
.B season1
with a count of eight, an empty file
.RB ( 0 ),
a broken link
.RB ( \-> X ),
a world-writable directory
.RB ( W ),
one 879K track marked as relatively large, and the ignored object file and the
dotfile counted in the summary but not shown.
.SS "The things that are usually hidden"
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sl -a --width 100 | tail -4
  dev              empty.txt0 <NOW     track 03.mp3 <NOW          dotfile
  main.c <NOW      image               video                      .hidden <NOW
  main.o0 <NOW     cover.png <NOW      season1/8 <NOW
  17 files (2.6M), 2 directories (8 files)

$ sl --ignore 2 --width 100 | tail -3
  main.c <NOW      image               video
  main.o0 <NOW     cover.png <NOW      season1/8 <NOW
  16 files (2.6M) + 1 .dotfile, 2 directories (8 files)
.EE
.RE
.PP
Level 3 (the default) hides both;
.B \\-\\-ignore 2
brings back the object file, level 1 also the dotfile, level 0 is
.BR \\-a .
.SS "Only what stands out"
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sl -o --width 100
  world/0W <NOW    util.c <NOW        audio                video
  build            doc                track 01.mp3 <NOW    season1/8 <NOW
  Makefile <NOW    empty.txt0 <NOW    track 02.mp3 <NOW    other
  dev              image              track 03.mp3 <NOW    README.md <NOW
  main.c <NOW      cover.png <NOW
.EE
.RE
.SS "Series, and why they sometimes do not collapse"
Fresh files carry
.B <NOW
marks, and a marked run is left alone \\- this is the original\\'s rule:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sl season1
  video                        episode 03.mkv <NOW    episode 06.mkv <NOW
   episode 01.mkv 244K <NOW    episode 04.mkv <NOW    episode 07.mkv <NOW
  episode 02.mkv <NOW          episode 05.mkv <NOW    episode 08.mkv <NOW
  8 files (1.9M)
.EE
.RE
.PP
Let the clock move on (here: give the files an old timestamp) and the same eight
names become one line:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ touch -d 2020-01-01 season1/*
$ sl season1
  video
   episode 01.mkv 244K <2020...8
  8 files (1.9M)
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B \\-\\-series 0
shows every name:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sl --series 0 season1
  video                        episode 02.mkv   episode 04.mkv   episode 06.mkv   episode 08.mkv
   episode 01.mkv 244K <2020   episode 03.mkv   episode 05.mkv   episode 07.mkv
  8 files (1.9M)
.EE
.RE
.SS "Shared prefixes"
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sl --one --prefix 4
  dev
   myproject-notes.txt 2B <NOW
  doc
  myproject-plan.txt <NOW
           -todo.txt <NOW
  other.txt <NOW
.EE
.RE
.PP
The prefix is replaced by spaces, so the column still lines up and only the
differing tails are read. It can also elide more than you expect \\- with
.B "track 01.mp3"
and
.B "track 02.mp3"
in one group, the shared
.B "track 0"
goes and the names read
.B 01.mp3
and
.BR 02.mp3 .
.SS "Cutting long names"
With ten or more names in a group and room for two columns, names are rewritten
before they are cut: articles are dropped, prepositions become a slash.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sl --width 120
  doc                                                          Making/Documentary about Sea/007/series.txt0 <54s
    Making/Documentary about Sea/001/series.txt0 0B <54s       Making/Documentary about Sea/008/series.txt0 <54s

$ sl --width 120 --shorten 0 | head -2
  doc
   The Making of a Documentary about the Sea volume 001 of the series.txt0 0B <54s
.EE
.RE
.PP
Same files, a narrower terminal, so the middle of each name is elided as well
and
.B \\-\\-ellipsis
picks the character:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sl --width 100 --ellipsis '»'
  doc                                                Making/Documentary about Sea/00»ries.txt0 <54s
    Making/Documentary about Se»ries.txt0 0B <54s    Making/Documentary about Sea/00»ries.txt0 <54s
.EE
.RE
.SS "Sorting by size and by time"
Four notes and three images, with the sizes and the timestamps deliberately
different:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sl \-\-one \-\-sort size .
   aaa/1 <NOW
   zzz/5 <NOW
  image
   c.png 586K
-> a.png 195K -2y
   b.png 29K
  other
   draft.md 293K
   readme.md 88K
   notes.md 4.9K
   todo.md 1B
  7 files (1.2M), 2 directories (6 files)
.EE
.RE
.PP
Every size is shown although
.B \-\-relsize
was not given: that is the implication of
.BR \-\-sort size .
The two directories are still in name order, even though
.B zzz
holds five entries and
.B aaa
one, because their size is a count and not a weight. The same directory sorted
by modification time:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sl \-\-one \-\-sort time .
  aaa/1 <NOW
  zzz/5 <NOW
  image
   c.png 586K
  b.png
->a.png \-2y
  other
  todo.md
  readme.md
  notes.md
  draft.md
  7 files (1.2M), 2 directories (6 files)
.EE
.RE
.PP
and by access time, which is a different order again:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sl \-\-one \-\-sort atime .
  aaa/1 <NOW
  zzz/5 <NOW
  image
->a.png \-2y
   c.png 586K
  b.png
  other
  notes.md
  todo.md
  readme.md
  draft.md
  7 files (1.2M), 2 directories (6 files)
.EE
.RE
.PP
In a grid the same order has to be read downwards, column by column:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sl \-\-width 100 \-\-sort size .
   aaa/1 <NOW    image        -> a.png 195K -2y    other              readme.md 88K     todo.md 1B
   zzz/5 <NOW     c.png 586K     b.png 29K          draft.md 293K     notes.md 4.9K
  7 files (1.2M), 2 directories (6 files)
.EE
.RE
.PP
Read the first column top to bottom (
.BR aaa ,
.BR zzz ),
then the second (
.BR image ,
.BR "c.png 586K" ),
then the third, and so on: the sizes keep falling, 586K, 195K, 29K, 293K, 88K,
4.9K, 1B.
Use
.B \-1
when the order is the point.
.SS "A single-child chain"
.B \\-\\-expando
is not recursion: it unfolds
.BR project/project-1.0/src
style chains.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sl --expando 0 project
  build          dev
  README <NOW     src/3 <NOW
  1 file (2B), 1 directory (3 files)

$ sl project
  build          dev
  README <NOW     src/3 <NOW
  1 file (2B), 1 directory (3 files)

autoexpand src/:
  deep/1 <NOW    dev                util.c <NOW
                  main.c 2B <NOW
  2 files (4B), 1 directory (1 file)

autoexpand deep/:
  dev
   a.c 2B <NOW
.EE
.RE
.SS "Your own marks: the column eza has for git"
The command runs in the directory being listed, with the entry\\'s path as $1
and its name as $2; its first line is used, cut to 24 characters. The trailing
time mark below depends on the clock.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sl --annotate 'git status --porcelain -- "$1" | head -1'
  doc                        tracked.txtM tracked.txt 8B <24s
  new.txt?? new.txt <24s
  2 files (12B) + 1 .dotfile
.EE
.RE
.PP
One process per entry is fine for a handful of names. For a directory use
.BR \\-\\-annotate-batch :
it receives every path on standard input and must answer with one line per path.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sl --annotate-batch 'while IFS= read -r p; do
      if [ -f "$p" ]; then echo "$(stat -c%s "$p")b"; else echo dir; fi
  done'
  world/0W dir <19s    empty.txt0 0b <19s
  build                image
  Makefile 5b <19s     cover.png 40000b <19s
  dev                  audio
  main.c 26b <19s       track 01.mp3 900000b 879K <19s
  util.c 26b <19s      track 02.mp3 900000b <19s
.EE
.RE
.SS "Other things worth knowing"
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sl --rule '\.bak$ -> archive' --rule '(?i)^(notes|todo)'   # force groups
$ sl --autosearch '(?i)key|password' ~/Downloads             # highlight matches
$ sl --relchange 50 --relsize 20 --width 100                 # mark more than usual
$ sl --no-config --k1000                                     # ignore the config, decimal units
$ sl --title --nosfx                                         # prettify names, drop suffixes
.EE
.RE
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Success, even if some of the named files did not exist (they are reported on
standard error, as
.B ls
does).
.TP
.B 1
A command line or configuration error.
.SH "DIFFERENCES FROM THE TCL ORIGINAL"
The grouping rules, warning marks, relative thresholds, series collapsing and
layout heuristics are reproduced from Thomas A. Phelps' script (v1.1.3,
revision 1.89, February 2012). The deliberate differences are:
.IP \(bu 2
No Tcl interpreter: the startup file
.I ~/.sl.tcl
is not read, and
.B perfile
/
.B vc-*
are replaced by
.BR \-\-annotate .
Configuration lives in
.IR ~/.config/sl/config .
.IP \(bu 2
Directory entries are sorted by name before processing, so the listing is
deterministic. The original inherited the order from the file system, which
means that when several entries tie on size and time, a different one may be
picked as the \(lqrepresentative\(rq of the group.
.IP \(bu 2
Colours can be switched off with
.BR \-\-no-color ,
.B NO_COLOR
and
.BR LS_COLORS ,
none of which the Tcl version had.
.IP \(bu 2
Character widths follow Unicode, so CJK names and emoji line up in columns;
the original assumed one column per character.
.IP \(bu 2
The Apple II group knows the disk image formats invented after 2012
.RB ( .woz ", " .hdv ", " .d13 ", " .edsk ", ...).
.IP \(bu 2
A directory is classified by the real type of its entries rather than by \[lq]no
suffix means subdirectory\[rq], and a name series needs one run of digits instead
of any single difference; see COMPATIBILITY for the two flags that restore the
original behaviour.
.IP \(bu 2
Not ported: the RCS/CVS version-control marks and the trick of restoring access
times with
.BR touch (1).
.SH BUGS
The series marker
.RB ( ...7 )
is appended to the size/time annotation of the first member of the run, so the
number of entries is printed where a size would be expected. Inherited from the
original.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-legacy-series
the detection is as generous as in the original: two names that differ in a
single letter count as a series.
.PP
Relative marking needs at least a few entries to be meaningful; in a directory
of two files everything is \(lqrelatively large\(rq and nothing is.
